# ██╗  ██╗██████╗  ██████╗ ███████╗
# ██║ ██╔╝██╔══██╗██╔═══██╗██╔════╝
# █████╔╝ ██║  ██║██║   ██║███████╗
# ██╔═██╗ ██║  ██║██║   ██║╚════██║
# ██║  ██╗██████╔╝╚██████╔╝███████║
# ╚═╝  ╚═╝╚═════╝  ╚═════╝ ╚══════╝
# ---------------------------------
#   What opens a file on the CONSOLE desktop.
#
# The console session exports XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=KDOS-Console:KDOS, so this
# file is consulted for it and never under the compositor — which is the whole
# point: a picture opens in `timg` inside a terminal here and in a boxed viewer
# there, and neither desktop edits the other's choices.
#
# AT /etc/xdg AND NOT IN A HOME. A person's own choice, wherever they make it,
# lives in their config directory and is searched first; a shipped default that
# outranked it would be a chooser that appears to do nothing. This is the
# fallback, which is what a distribution default is.
#
# ONLY WHAT THE TWO DESKTOPS ANSWER DIFFERENTLY. A type both would open the
# same way — a directory in `mc` — belongs in the plain `mimeapps.list` beside
# this file, or the same decision is written twice and drifts.
#
# EVERY ROW NAMES AN ENTRY THAT IS INSTALLED. An id nothing provides falls
# through to the next candidate silently, so a row for a program that is not
# here is a row nobody can tell is dead.
# ---------------------------------

[Default Applications]
text/plain=micro.desktop
text/markdown=micro.desktop
text/x-shellscript=micro.desktop
application/json=micro.desktop
application/xml=micro.desktop
# THE THREE `libkimg` DECODES GO TO THE VIEWER, and the rest to `timg`.
# `kdos-pix` fits, zooms, pans and steps through the folder; `timg` prints a
# picture once and exits, which is the right answer only for a format the
# viewer cannot open. A row here for a type `kdos-pix` refuses would be a
# viewer that opens and says it cannot read the file.
image/png=kdos-pix.desktop
image/jpeg=kdos-pix.desktop
image/webp=kdos-pix.desktop
image/gif=timg.desktop
image/bmp=timg.desktop
image/tiff=timg.desktop
text/csv=visidata.desktop
# A SPREADSHEET GOES TO sc-im AND A DATA FILE TO visidata, and the difference is
# measured rather than tasted. sc-im reads xlsx in C — libzip and libxml2, an
# `open_xlsx` symbol, `-DXLSX` in the binary — so it opens one with no Python
# in the way. visidata reaches the same file only through openpyxl, which is
# why that port exists: `vd` on an xlsx without it answers "package `openpyxl`
# not installed" and writes nothing.
#
# IT READS ONE AND CANNOT WRITE ONE. sc-im's xlsx EXPORT is gated on
# libxlsxwriter, which is not a port here, so saving goes back out as .sc, .csv
# or .ods — see the gaps page.
application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet=sc-im.desktop
# A .docx ON THE CONSOLE, where the office suite that would open it is a
# Wayland application and cannot run. `doxx` renders it read-only in the
# terminal; under the compositor the plain table's answer stands.
application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document=doxx.desktop
# AND A PAGE ALREADY ON THE DISK. Without this row `text/html` is claimed only
# by the browser box's generated entry, which is a Wayland client: opening a
# saved page on the console answered "could not compose box 'app.firefox-esr'".
# Nothing can reorder its way out of that — there is one candidate, and the
# console's own browser is reachable only by a row like this one, because
# w3m.desktop carries no MimeType line and /usr/share/applications has no
# mimeinfo.cache.
text/html=w3m.desktop
# A LINK, ON A DESKTOP WITH NO COMPOSITOR. `w3m` and not `lynx` for the reason
# it is this image's text browser at all: it lays tables out on the grid, and a
# page that is nothing but tables is readable. The entry is `Terminal=true`, so
# the opener wraps it in `kdos-term` rather than starting a browser nobody can
# see. Under the compositor these types are the browser box's, which is why
# they are here and not in the plain list beside this file.
x-scheme-handler/http=w3m.desktop
x-scheme-handler/https=w3m.desktop
